1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,500
 In the post-war period in the 1950s, this is a bit of an aside, there was a realization

2
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:14,960
 by the, certainly the federal government in the United States, that academics were kind

3
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:22,420
 of useful, right? I mean, the people that really had won the war for them were academics.

4
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:27,280
 Not just computer people, but also people that built designed airplanes and radar and

5
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:32,820
 things like that. And so there's a sense that the safest country was the country that

6
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:38,220
 had the smartest people. And in many ways, the entire US educational system was built

7
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:48,020
 to find really bright scientists and educate them very effectively. And so there was a

8
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:54,980
 real boom in building science departments, National Science Foundation was created right

9
00:00:54,980 --> 00:01:03,020
 after the war. And so the United States and other governments really greatly invested

10
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:07,700
 in research. And there's this movie, Beautiful Mind, and I've got the URL there at the bottom.

11
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:11,460
 It's not required, but I love the movie. And it gives you a sense, in particular the scene

12
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:18,540
 that I'm pointing to, gives you a sense of how mathematicians were in many ways treated

13
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:22,140
 like celebrities. So I'll pause just for a minute in case you want to pause this video

14
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:34,020
 and then go take a look at the John Nash video.

15
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:40,180
 So of course, John Nash was a real person. He received his PhD in mathematics at Princeton

16
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:46,380
 at 22 years old. He was a prodigy. He was on the mathematics faculty at MIT in 1951

17
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:54,120
 through 1958. And he had adult onset schizophrenia right before he was 30 years old. And it was

18
00:01:54,120 --> 00:02:02,140
 quite a severe case. And he spent a number of years in sort of mental institutions, homeless,

19
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:10,220
 et cetera. But in his work in Princeton, in an area of economics called game theory, he

20
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:15,420
 did something that was quite innovative. And it really revolutionized in many ways our

21
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:26,340
 understanding of economics. And he got his schizophrenia under control in the mid '90s.

22
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:32,260
 And he was awarded the Nobel Prize for economics in 1994. And so this is just kind of this

23
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:38,620
 moment where all this stuff was blossoming in the '40s and the '50s.

24
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:48,060
 Now in the 1960s, we started seeing a different way of computing. This was moving from the

25
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:53,660
 research into the mathematics of building computers to the applications of the computers.

26
00:02:53,660 --> 00:03:02,220
 And we needed to have connectivity because you would build a computer and in 1955, there

27
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:07,660
 might be one computer available to a university researcher in the entire state. And so you

28
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:14,300
 had to connect them together. And so we would often end up with these things we called terminals

29
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:20,620
 in our office. And they would use these dial up modems. And they would connect perhaps

30
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:28,620
 for a local call to a local computer or even a long distance call, a very expensive long

31
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:34,060
 distance call to a remote computer. But these computers were so central and so precious

32
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:39,140
 that we didn't care about the cost of phones. And we couldn't share them. I mean, everybody

33
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:44,620
 didn't have a computer the way they do today. And so there was a couple of different models.

34
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:51,180
 One is that you had people doing their work connecting to computers over these modems.

35
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:55,380
 And then more rarely, you had computer to computer connections using what are called

36
00:03:55,380 --> 00:04:02,900
 lease lines. These lease lines were very expensive. They were kind of like making a long distance

37
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:08,700
 phone call 24 hours a day, seven days a week. They were rarely used in academic situations

38
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:14,780
 because they were so expensive. It was more common for a bank to use them to say move

39
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:19,020
 all their data from their branch locations to the central locations once a day. They

40
00:04:19,020 --> 00:04:26,520
 were generally slow and very expensive. And they were justifiable in certain situations.

41
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:31,940
 And so that's kind of how our world was. We had something on our desk. And then we used

42
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:36,420
 that to connect through the phone lines to a central computer that was shared with many

43
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:42,380
 different people. We called it time sharing. And so-- oops. So this next video that I want

44
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:48,180
 to show you, just the first part of it, you don't have to watch the whole thing, is kind

45
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:54,900
 of in the '60s and '70s when I became involved in computing, this was kind of the way that

46
00:04:54,900 --> 00:05:01,820
 we perceived our computers. We would dial them up. And they made these clunking noises

47
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:09,000
 that this teletype that you're going to see in this video is a World War II artifact basically.

48
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,580
 And my first programming, personally, was on this kind of a computer, making this exact

49
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:18,740
 noise that you're going to hear in this video. And I think that the key to emphasize here

50
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:27,380
 is that it's kind of fun to look back at this old guy with his horny rim glasses. But we

51
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:31,240
 really were enjoying ourselves. And you think, well, I got this fancy cell phone and it does

52
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:36,460
 all these wonderful things. And I place Angry Birds on it. And weren't these people not

53
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:41,500
 having fun? And the answer is we were having a great time. We were connecting, communicating,

54
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:46,220
 calculating, computing, doing all these wonderful things. We just were doing it sort of more

55
00:05:46,220 --> 00:05:52,140
 slowly with simpler equipment. But even from the beginning in the '60s and the '70s, people

56
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:58,340
 talking to people were an important part of the use of this technology. So take a quick

57
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:05,340
 look at this video, and then come on back.

58
00:06:28,340 --> 00:06:46,160
 I'm going to show you the use of the Michigan Terminal System, a large computing system

59
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:53,920
 at the University of Michigan Computing Center, using the IBM Model 36067. We will be using

60
00:06:53,920 --> 00:07:00,680
 a standard teletype, and we will be dialing in through the ordinary University Centrix

61
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:08,720
 telephone system. This system can be used by a telephone system anywhere in the world,

62
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:14,300
 as long as one can dial directly into the computer. These teletypes are not wired in,

63
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:20,520
 but use the ordinary telephone system. We start by pushing the originate button, which

64
00:07:20,520 --> 00:07:26,640
 turns on the teletype. Then we will dial using the regular telephone dial here. Now, there

65
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:33,800
 is a handset, which is available with every teletype system. You can dial this number,

66
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:38,560
 if you like, with the handset. Either one works. But as soon as you are connected to

67
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:44,360
 the computer, do not use the handset for any purpose at all. Now I'll push the originate

68
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:50,640
 button. I can control the speaker volume with this little knob, and so you can hear it.

69
00:07:50,640 --> 00:08:04,400
 Now I will dial the number, 763-0300, or in the Centrix system, just 30300. And now the

70
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:11,840
 computer is coming in. I will turn the volume down. The response from the computer is the

71
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:17,240
 identification of the system. The "who are you?" is the standard teletype communication

72
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:22,640
 identification. And the teletype itself answers with its own built-in code number. In this

73
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:30,160
 case, the teletype at the TV center. Now I must sign on to the computer and identify

74
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:35,200
 myself. The number sign at the beginning of the line is the indication that the Michigan

75
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:40,960
 terminal system is waiting for me to communicate something to it. The first thing I must do

76
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:48,320
 is issue the sign-on command. All commands in the system start with a dollar sign. Now

77
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:54,540
 the keyboard on the teletype is very much like the keyboard of a typewriter, including

78
00:08:54,540 --> 00:09:00,560
 a shift key for the characters on the top of the key. We do not have upper and lower

79
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:06,720
 case letters, however, so the shift key is only used to get an additional set of characters.

80
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:13,280
 And in this case, the dollar sign is a character above the number 4. Now I will use the word

81
00:09:13,280 --> 00:09:21,560
 sign-on, which is the official command, S-I-G-N-O-N. There must be a space, at least one, after

82
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:26,400
 every command. And now I will give my own personal computing center number, which happens

83
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:35,360
 to be W010. Each user of this system is issued a computing center identification number through

84
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:40,800
 his instructor if he's a student or through the department for faculty. Now this line

85
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:48,880
 has been typed. It is being held by the computer, but I can, if I see any errors in it, do some

86
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:54,240
 editing and correcting. We'll see that later. I am satisfied with this line, and now I will

87
00:09:54,240 --> 00:10:00,880
 give the end-of-line character, which must be given after every line, and I will remind

88
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:06,920
 you each time that I'm doing it for a while. The character for ending a line uses the control

89
00:10:06,920 --> 00:10:12,880
 key on the keyboard, which is a special key which indicates that I want to use even additional

90
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:17,960
 characters besides those that are added by the use of the shift key. I hold the control

91
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:24,440
 key down, and while I hold it down, I can hit one of a number of other keys. And in

92
00:10:24,440 --> 00:10:31,480
 this case, I will use the control with the letter Q. It's possible to use the letter S also,

93
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:35,660
 but that's right next to another key which disconnects the teletype, so I recommend using

94
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:48,560
 Q. I will use control and Q to terminate the line. And now I'm asked to give my private

95
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:55,840
 password. I'll give an incorrect password, just some nonsense characters, and of course,

96
00:10:55,840 --> 00:11:06,040
 end-of-line because I must indicate that I'm satisfied with that. I'm prompted that I better

97
00:11:06,040 --> 00:11:11,060
 give another password, and now since I intend to give the correct password, I will turn

98
00:11:11,060 --> 00:11:16,760
 off the printing so that no one can see what mine is. To turn off printing, we use this

99
00:11:16,760 --> 00:11:27,000
 switch which is labeled HDX and FDX for half-duplex and full-duplex. We normally operate the teletype

100
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:33,560
 in half-duplex mode. If I push it to full-duplex, printing is suppressed. And now I will give

101
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:42,520
 my correct password, push to half-duplex again, and end-of-line character, control Q, and

102
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:48,600
 it is accepted by the system, and I am now signed on. The information that's coming out

103
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:51,840
 here is time and date of the previous sign-on.

